@* CATCH-ALL ROUTE — the * in {*slug} is what separates this from an ordinary parameter.
   A single-segment param like {id:int} captures ONE segment (/products/42 → id=42); a
   catch-all captures the ENTIRE remaining path, slashes and all, into one string
   (/docs/api/v2/auth → slug = "api/v2/auth"). Reach for it when the path depth isn't known
   ahead of time — documentation trees, wiki paths, file-browser URLs. *@
@page "/docs/{*slug}"

<h3>Documentation</h3>

<p>Requested path: <code>@Slug</code></p>

@code {
    // Nullable on purpose: /docs with nothing after it leaves Slug null (the catch-all matched
    // zero segments). Any deeper path lands here as a single slash-joined string.
    [Parameter] public string? Slug { get; set; }
}
